  "model": "s2.1-pro-free",
  "text": "“I miss it. My work hours changed before the storm. I used to finish before the storytelling began. Now I wait for the last boat and help everyone ashore. By the time I could reach the garden, they would have gone home. This page has made more trips to the dock than to Grandma.”\n\n“This is for my grandmother,” he said. “My sister made it, but she’s too ill to come. I promised my sister I’d keep it safe.” He turned it a little, as if there might be an angle from which the wing still looked whole.\n\nI fetched some tape from the dock office. When I came back, he was holding the torn edges near each other. “Like this?” he asked. I bent down so I could see. “That looks right. Keep them there while I lay the tape.”\n\nEveryone had stepped off the last boat except a boy holding a paper bird. The operator waited while I went back to see whether he needed a hand. He stood very still, though the boat moved gently against the dock.\n\nTogether, we mended the wing. He held the paper steady while I laid the tape across the tear. We smoothed it gently. The folds his sister had made were still there. So was a small shiny line where we had worked.\n\nAs we reached the dock, the tune was nearly finished. The boy could see people gathering their things, but he kept playing. The other passengers waited for the final note before stepping ashore.\n\nThe boy started again. This time, they played together. When he drew breath, the whistle carried on for a note, and he came back in beside it. A passenger who had been talking lowered her voice.\n\nA whistle answered from the back of the boat. An older passenger had recognised the tune. The boy stopped. The whistle stopped too. They looked at one another across the seated passengers.",
